WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.310
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:06.260
Please meet NeuroSAT.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.750
The name of this technique tells us what it
is about, the neuro part means that it is

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:16.660
a neural network-based learning method, and
the SAT part means that it is able to solve

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.830
satisfiability problems.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:23.949
This is a family of problems where we are
given a logic formula, and we have to decide

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:29.769
whether these variables can be chosen in a
way such that this expression comes out true.

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:35.210
That, of course, sounds quite nebulous, so
let’s have a look at a simple example - this

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:42.320
formula says that F is true if A is true and
at the same time not B is true.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:49.420
So if we choose A to be true and B as false,
this expression is also true, or in other

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:52.020
words, this problem is satisfied.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.840
Getting a good solution for SAT is already
great for solving many problems involving

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:02.539
logic, however, the more interesting part
is that it can help us solve an enormous set

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:08.360
of other problems, for instance, ones that
involve graphs describing people in social

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.219
networks, and many others that you see here.

00:01:11.219 --> 00:01:16.109
This can be done by performing something that
mathematicians like to call polynomial-time

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:22.819
reduction or Karp-reduction, which means that
many other problems that seem completely different

00:01:22.819 --> 00:01:25.529
can be converted into a SAT problem.

00:01:25.529 --> 00:01:30.789
In short, if you can solve SAT well, you can
solve all of these problems well.

00:01:30.789 --> 00:01:36.020
This is one of the amazing revelations I learned
about during my mathematical curriculum.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:41.669
The only problem is that when trying to solve
big and complex SAT problems, we can often

00:01:41.669 --> 00:01:48.950
not do much better than random guessing, which,
for some of most nasty ones, takes so long

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.819
that it practically is never going to finish.

00:01:51.819 --> 00:01:57.649
And get this, interestingly, this work presents
us with a neural network that is able to solve

00:01:57.649 --> 00:02:04.270
problems of this form, but not like this tiny-tiny
baby problem, but much bigger ones.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:07.079
And, this really shouldn’t be possible.

00:02:07.079 --> 00:02:08.500
Here’s why.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:12.200
To train a neural network, we require training
data.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:18.459
The input is a problem definition and the
output is whether this problem is satisfiable.

00:02:18.459 --> 00:02:22.450
And we can stop right here, because here lies
our problem.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:27.370
This doesn’t really make any sense, because
we just said that it is difficult to solve

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:29.189
big SAT problems.

00:02:29.189 --> 00:02:30.940
And here comes the catch!

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:36.900
This neural network learns from SAT problems
that are small enough to be solved by traditional

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.560
handcrafted methods.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.680
We can create arbitrarily many training examples
with these solvers, albeit these are all small

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:45.680
ones.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.849
And that’s not it, there are three key factors
here that make this technique really work.

00:02:50.849 --> 00:02:54.579
One, it learns from only single-bit supervision.

00:02:54.579 --> 00:02:59.299
This means that the output that we talked
about is only yes or no.

00:02:59.299 --> 00:03:01.310
It isn’t shown the solution itself.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.990
That’s all the algorithm learns from.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:09.950
Two, when we request a solution from the neural
network, it not only tells us the same binary

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:14.129
yes-no answer that was used to teach it, but
it can go beyond that, and when the problem

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:19.590
is satisfiable, it will almost always provide
us with the exact solution.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:25.270
It is not only able to tell us whether the
problem can be solved, but it almost always

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:27.560
provides a possible solution as well.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.160
That is indeed remarkable.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.930
This image may be familiar from the thumbnail,
and here you can see how the neural network’s

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:40.999
inner representation of how these variables
change over time as it sees a satisfiable

00:03:40.999 --> 00:03:46.170
and unsatisfiable problem and how it comes
to its own conclusions.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:51.560
And three, when we ask the neural network
for a solution, it is able to defeat problems

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:56.689
that are larger and more difficult than the
ones it has trained on.

00:03:56.689 --> 00:04:03.019
So this means that we train it on simple problems
that we can solve ourselves, and using these

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:08.430
as training data, it will be able to solve
much harder problems that we can’t solve

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:09.489
ourselves.

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:16.019
This is crucial, because otherwise, this neural
network would only be as good as the handcrafted

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:17.810
algorithm used to train it.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:20.340
Which in other words, is not useful at all.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.049
Isn’t this amazing?

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:27.680
I will note that there are handcrafted algorithms
that are able to match, and often outperform

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:34.330
NeuroSAT, however, those took decades of research
work to invent, whereas this is a learning-based

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:40.690
technique that just looks at as little information
as the problem definition and whether it is

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:45.880
satisfiable, and it is able to come up with
a damn good algorithm by itself.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.800
What a time to be alive.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.240
